Optimal Solution - Hierholzer’s algorithm to find lexicographically smallest Eulerian path in directed graph.
                   This is an Eulerian path problem — use post-order DFS and reverse the result to reconstruct itinerary.

class Solution {
    // Adjacency list: source -> list of destinations
    Map<String, List<String>> graph;
    // Stores final itinerary (Eulerian path)
    List<String> itinerary;
    // DFS using Hierholzer’s algorithm
    public void dfs(String from) {
        // Get all destinations from current airport
        List<String> to = graph.get(from);
        // While there are outgoing edges
        while (to != null && !to.isEmpty()) {
            // Remove last destination (smallest lexicographically after reverse sort)
            String nextTo = to.remove(to.size() - 1);
            // Visit next airport
            dfs(nextTo);
        }
        // Add airport to itinerary AFTER visiting all edges
        // (Post-order insertion)
        itinerary.add(from);
    }
    public List<String> findItinerary(List<List<String>> tickets) {
        graph = new HashMap<>();
        itinerary = new ArrayList<>();
        // Build adjacency list
        for (List<String> ticket : tickets) {
            graph.computeIfAbsent(ticket.get(0),
                    k -> new ArrayList<>())
                    .add(ticket.get(1));
        }
        // Sort destinations in reverse lexicographical order
        // So we can pop smallest lexicographical destination
        for (List<String> to : graph.values()) {
            to.sort(Collections.reverseOrder());
        }
        // Start DFS from JFK
        dfs("JFK");
        // Reverse because nodes were added post-order
        Collections.reverse(itinerary);
        return itinerary;
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(E log E).
Space Complexity :- O(V + E).
